{"actionsRun":["rm -rf .peer-lab/spawn-one-watch","Read peer-sessions/SKILL.md","ls -la lab lab/x lab/y 2>&1; ls peer-sessions/scripts; git -C lab/x rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>&1; git -C lab/y rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>&1","grep -n -i -A3 "\-\-dirs" peer-sessions/references/placement.md | head -40; python3 peer-sessions/scripts/spawn-fleet.py --help 2>&1 | head -60","python3 peer-sessions/scripts/spawn-fleet.py --placement workspace x-readme:lab/x y-readme:lab/y","python3 peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py --name x-readme --details; python3 peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py --name y-readme --details"],"placement":"workspace","tellUser":"Both Claude peers are running in a new herdr workspace, wF1. `x-readme` is in lab/x (pane wF1:p1) and `y-readme` is in lab/y (pane wF1:p2). Neither directory existed, so the spawn script created them. They aren't git checkouts, so I named the sessions after the work. Per your instruction, I haven't sent any briefs.

One problem to check before briefing: `peer-addr.py` shows both sessions on the same PID (50938) and the same socket (`uds:/tmp/pslab-6fjglrpa/s.sock`), though their session IDs differ. A `uds:` address may therefore not reach one specific peer. When briefing, use the herdr commands the script suggested (`herdr agent prompt x-readme ...` and `herdr agent prompt y-readme ...`) or the bare name plus its `[ref]`.

The fleet stays up until you ask me to stop it. The teardown the script printed is `kill 50938`, then `herdr workspace close wF1`, then `python3 ~/.claude/skills/peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py` to confirm. Because of the shared PID, run that last check to make sure both sessions are actually gone. To resume after a reboot, run `claude --resume x-readme` from lab/x or `claude --resume y-readme` from lab/y."}
